e £1,800 target by comparing the target to raw materials, but the brief target is “OEM unit cost” and the report’s own OEM transfer price is £2,283.56.\n   Evidence: “at a maximum OEM unit cost of £1,800.” and “Raw materials BoM … £1,801.09 … = OEM transfer price £2,283.56”\n   Root cause: Cost gate / `cost_target_evaluator.ts` compares target against `raw_materials_bom` instead of `oem_transfer_price`.\n   Fix: Change target comparator to `costStack.oemTransferPrice <= brief.maxOemUnitCost`; if the user target explicitly says “raw BoM”, only then compare against `rawMaterialsBom`.\n\n3. (severity HIGH) — Cost provenance / hidden scaling\n   Finding: The £1,801.09 BoM is not a grounded supplier BoM because every line is a web estimate and the report applies a 0.325 batch-economics scale factor despite having zero live quotes.\n   Evidence: “0 actual ~ 137 estimate ? 0 TBD” and “0 live distributor quotes · 137 web estimates · 0 TBD · Raw BoM scale factor 0.325” and “Scale factor: 0.325 — per-class batch-economics multiplier applied to distributor-quoted lines”\n   Root cause: Cost engine / `engine_c_costing.ts` applies the production scale factor to non-actual estimates and the wording incorrectly says it is applied to distributor-quoted lines.\n   Fix: Apply volume scaling only when `price_source == ACTUAL_DISTRIBUTOR_QUOTE`; for `WEB_ESTIMATE` show unscaled estimate plus a confidence band, or require an explicit `estimate_already_scaled=true` flag.\n\n4. (severity HIGH) — Apparent BoM increase cause\n   Finding: The apparent BoM increase from the previous iteration is likely caused by pricing all unresolved candidate parts instead of leaving unverifiable items as TBD or stripping them, so the report has more priced-but-unverified lines rather than better sourcing.\n   Evidence: “137 part lines · 11 modules … 0 actual … ~ 137 estimate … ? 0 TBD” and “The remaining ‘uncertain’ parts are kept because removing them would leave gaps in the bill of materials”\n   Root cause: Verification-to-cost handoff / `bom_verification_mapper.ts` allows `verification_status=UNCERTAIN` parts to enter the priced BoM as normal costed lines.\n   Fix: Set `price_included=false` for `UNCERTAIN` parts unless a verified catalogue match exists; render them in Appendix A only and exclude from target-gate calculations.\n\n5. (severity HIGH) — Implausible component prices\n   Finding: Several major refrigeration components are priced at physically impossible values, making both the £1,801 BoM and any £4,969 regression number untrustworthy.\n   Evidence: “R290 Scroll Compressor Copeland YP0135E-TFD-290 ~ £113.20” and “Fin-and-Tube Evaporator Kelvion KEV-EVP-8KW-R290 ~ £2.96” and “Brazed Plate Condenser SWEP B10-8KW-R290 ~ £2.96”\n   Root cause: Engine B cost-class curves / `part_price_estimator.ts` misclassifies high-value OEM refrigeration assemblies and then scales them down using generic class multipliers.\n   Fix: Add class-specific floor prices and sanity ranges for `compressor`, `evaporator_coil`, `brazed_plate_heat_exchanger`, `inverter_drive`, and `gas_detector`; fail the costing gate if an 8 kW compressor is below e.g. £250 or a heat exchanger below e.g. £80 at production volume.\n\n6. (severity MED) — Duplicate BoM physical parts\n   Finding: The BoM double-counts parts across functional modules, inflating cost and obscuring real assembly boundaries.\n   Evidence: “A ebm-papst evaporator fan motor (part G2E140-BL31-01…” and later “A ebm-papst ecm fan motor (part G2E140-BL31-01…”; also “A Danfoss filter drier core (part DML 164 R290…” and later “A Danfoss filter drier (part DML 164 R290…”\n   Root cause: Module decomposition / `bom_extractor.ts` treats every functional mention as a new physical line item and lacks manufacturer-part-number de-duplication.\n   Fix: Add a canonical physical-part table keyed by normalized `(manufacturer, part_number)`; if the same part appears in multiple modules, emit one BoM line with multiple `used_by_modules`.\n\n7. (severity MED) — Compressor assembly double-counting\n   Finding: The report buys a complete compressor and also separately buys internal compressor components such as scroll set, bearings, seals, thrust washer, and oil.\n   Evidence: “A Copeland r290 scroll compressor … in Cast iron shell, hardened steel scrolls” and “A Copeland scroll compression set … 2x NSK crankshaft bearing … 2x Copeland scroll tip seal … A Copeland thrust washer … A Emkarate compressor oil charge”\n   Root cause: BoM boundary handling / `assembly_boundary_classifier.ts` fails to mark the compressor as a sealed purchased assembly.\n   Fix: Mark `compressor` as `purchased_assembly=true`; suppress internal kinematic child parts from the procurement BoM unless `service_spares=true`.\n\n8. (severity HIGH) — R290 leak detector placement\n   Finding: The R290 leak detector is placed at the high point even though propane is heavier than air and should be detected near low accumulation points or forced ventilation paths.\n   Evidence: “A Dräger r290 catalytic gas sensor … certified to BS EN 378-3 (additional: topology: High point mount)” and “mounted at the high point of the compartment.”\n   Root cause: Refrigerant safety rulebase / `a3_refrigerant_rules.ts` uses a generic leak-detection placement rule instead of refrigerant-density-specific placement.\n   Fix: Add refrigerant property `vapour_density_relative_to_air`; for R290 set `detector_mounting = LOW_POINT_OR_DRAIN_PATH`, and fail the safety gate if generated text says “high point mount”.\n\n9. (severity HIGH) — R290 ignition-source control\n   Finding: The report incorrectly treats IP54 ingress protection as sufficient to prevent spark ignition inside an R290 refrigerant compartment.\n   Evidence: “All electrical components within the refrigerant compartment are rated to IP54 or higher to prevent spark generation in the event of minor leaks.”\n   Root cause: Safety compliance generator / `iec60335_2_40_checker.ts` conflates IP ingress rating with ignition-source elimination.\n   Fix: Replace the rule with an A3 ignition-source assessment: move relays/contactors/sparking devices outside the refrigerant compartment, use sealed/approved components where required, document ventilation, and require a pass/fail `ignition_source_assessment`.\n\n10. (severity HIGH) — Compressor still not procurement-compatible\n   Finding: The selected compressor is still not procurement-ready because the part is unverified and the physics critic flags the motor code as 3-phase 380–420 V, incompatible with a UK residential single-phase brief.\n   Evidence: “R290 scroll compressor Copeland YP0135E-TFD-290 … Reason for uncertainty” and “The compressor motor code ‘TFD’ (YP0135E-TFD-290) indicates a 3-phase 380-420V motor.”\n   Root cause: Compressor selector / `compressor_candidate_filter.ts` only checked refrigerant label text and did not validate datasheet existence, voltage, phase, frequency, or market availability.\n   Fix: Require verified compressor datasheet fields `{refrigerant: R290, supply: 230V, phase: 1, frequency: 50Hz, capacity_A7W35≈8kW}`; reject Copeland `TFD` motor-code candidates for UK domestic designs.\n\n11. (severity MED) — Pressure-safety architecture incomplete\n   Finding: The design discusses high-side overpressure but lists only a high-pressure switch, not a pressure relief valve or receiver relief device for the R290 circuit.\n   Evidence: “Typical mitigations: PED-rated pressure-relief valve sized to compressor displacement” and “The refrigerant safety valves include … a 30 bar high-pressure safety switch” and “A Danfoss high-pressure safety switch … rated 30 bar trip”\n   Root cause: Risk-to-BoM mitigation closure / `fmea_mitigation_mapper.ts` does not require each high-severity mitigation to map to an actual BoM item.\n   Fix: Add a mitigation-closure check: for `MEC-01 High-side pressure excursion`, require a verified PRV/rupture device or a justified sealed-system exemption with design-pressure calculation and notified-body rationale.\n\n12. (severity HIGH) — £1,800 target feasibility\n   Finding: A £1,800 OEM transfer price is not achievable for this listed 8 kW UK R290 monobloc architecture because the report’s own cost stack exceeds it and the raw BoM only appears near target through unverified, scaled estimates.\n   Evidence: “at a maximum OEM unit cost of £1,800.” and “= OEM transfer price £2,283.56” and “0 live distributor quotes · 137 web estimates”\n   Root cause: Product feasibility gate / `class_cost_feasibility.ts` lacks a parametric floor model for residential ASHPs and allows unverified estimates to satisfy a commercial target.\n   Fix: Add a class-level feasibility model for `heat-pump-residential-8kW-R290`: compute minimum credible raw BoM, factory COGS, and OEM transfer separately; mark £1,800 OEM as infeasible unless the architecture is reduced and verified supplier quotes support it.\n\n13. (severity HIGH) — Performance architecture unresolved\n   Finding: The system still cannot credibly deliver 8 kW because the selected fan motor is far below the airflow required for the evaporator load.\n   Evidence: “The fan motor specified is a 140 mm centrifugal blower rated at 80 W … An 8 kW thermal output heat pump requires approximately 3000 to 4300 m³/h of airflow … The G2E140 blower can only deliver <500 m³/h”\n   Root cause: Physics critic findings / `physics_critic_gate.ts` are rendered as manual notes but do not block the BoM or force regeneration.\n   Fix: Promote this critic rule to an enforcing gate: calculate required airflow from evaporator heat duty and allowable air ΔT, then reject fans below required `m3_per_h` at stated static pressure.\n\n14. (severity MED) — Supplier population does not verify BoM\n   Finding: The supplier section is populated with broad company archetypes, but it does not resolve or verify the actual BoM part numbers.\n   Evidence: “Caveat — candidates are surfaced from a 28,000-company database plus a scored web fallback” and “Total checked: 137 … Verified: 0”\n   Root cause: Supplier recommender / `supplier_cards.ts` is disconnected from part-level sourcing / `part_verifier.ts`.\n   Fix: Link supplier cards to specific BoM line coverage: require each recommended supplier to provide at least one verified manufacturer/part-number match or quote; otherwise label them “capability lead only” and do not count toward verified sourcing.","refusal":null,"reasoning":"**Evaluating cost references**\n\nI'm looking at the appendix, which shows a reference for the compressor priced at £348, while the Bill of Materials (BoM) shows £113.20 after scaling it by a factor of 0.325 post Engine B. Some items were misclassified as OEM subsystems, leading to inflated costs before scaling. The increase from 3955 to 4969 likely comes from adding components to address unverified parts. The report has 137 lines, suggesting a need to note potential catastrophic cost underestimates, like heat exchangers at £2.96.**Assessing cost discrepancies**\n\nI'm noticing that the target raw cost of £1800 was only achieved due to poor scaling and misclassification. It's puzzling because the brief states that the maximum OEM unit cost should be £1,800. However, the raw Bill of Materials (BoM) shows £1801, while the OEM transfer cost of £2283.56 exceeds this target. There seems to be a significant discrepancy that needs to be addressed for accurate financial assessment.**Evaluating refrigeration safety**\n\nI need to address some ventilation issues and calculate the charge for a 0.8 kg R290 system, considering leak sensors. I’ve seen evidence about charge limits per occupied area, but without proper calculations. If there’s a leak, the flammable cloud risk is concerning, especially with the pressure details. The MAWP seems too low for R290, and I need to check for critical pressures and missing relief valves in the design. Overall, there seem to be significant risks that require further findings.**Analyzing compressor compatibility**\n\nI’m looking into the liquid receiver for a full charge pump-down with a 0.8 kg propane system. While outdoor service might be fine, the receiver definitely needs a pressure relief valve. The chosen compressor, YP0135E-TFD-290, is supposed to be compatible, but its fabrication status is still unverified from the appendix. There’s a mention of using R290-rated compressors, but even with chemistry hazards addressed, electrical incompatibilities still pose a concern that needs to be resolved.**Analyzing cost discrepancies**\n\nIt seems the report doesn’t list £4969 due to issues with the cost regression investigation, which is blocked by an inconsistent cost layer. I find quotes of £1801, but the appendix remains unverified. I need to fix the summary aggregator to use the report's cost layer rather than an external state. I also wonder if the £4969 is the total unscaled amount, and I need to explore the differences between raw and scaled estimates.**Clarifying scaling factors**\n\nI realize I shouldn’t apply the hidden factor to web estimates unless they are calibrated. The displayed unit price really needs to include scaling and provenance, which might explain why the report shows an artificially inflated £1800. I’m curious about why the price increased – perhaps earlier iterations didn’t scale correctly. The report highlights that there are \"0 live distributor quotes\" and \"137 web estimates,\" which seems contradictory since it mentions a scale factor of 0.325. I need to look into this further.**Analyzing cost reporting issues**\n\nI'm listing findings related to cost reporting inconsistencies. The first issue shows a high discrepancy between the report's claim of £4969 and the PDF's number of £1801.09 and 137 unverified parts. This suggests an outdated cost layer. Next is the misreading of the OEM unit cost, where the report shows £1800 instead of the correct £2283.56. Lastly, estimates are affected by a misapplied scale factor that led to inaccurate figures. I need to ensure that corrections in `runSummary.ts` and `cost_gate.ts` are implemented.**Addressing cost adjustments**\n\nI need to fix the issue where if the `price_source` isn't ACTUAL, the scale shouldn't be applied. I also need to store both the base unit cost and the scaled cost while ensuring confidence in the data. I’m wondering why costs have increased—could it be because estimates were pulled from 60 TBD? I should also mention that there's a high volume multiplier of 100k, as noted in the appendix stating \"annual volume=100,000.\" Identifying the root cause is essential here.**Evaluating technical issues**\n\nI'm looking at an evidence appendix that hasn’t been verified and a quote from a physics critic. The main problem seems to be about the compressor selection not validating the datasheet or motor. I need to fix the selector to restrict it to verified R290 single-phase 230V and parse the motor code to ensure everything aligns correctly.  There’s a lot to consider here, and I want to make sure I get it right!**Addressing fan performance issues**\n\nI’ve noticed that the fan being undersized and the input being truncated are critical since they can't support 8 kW. It's not just a cost issue; it’s major. I need to fix the fan selection calculation for airflow and ensure that any quotes I include are verbatim. My response shouldn't be too long, aiming for a concise summary of findings. I plan on including specific code changes and addressing the cost increase from £3955 to £4969 due to unverified estimates.","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.summary","summary":"**Evaluating cost references**\n\nI'm looking at the appendix, which shows a reference for the compressor priced at £348, while the Bill of Materials (BoM) shows £113.20 after scaling it by a factor of 0.325 post Engine B. Some items were misclassified as OEM subsystems, leading to inflated costs before scaling. The increase from 3955 to 4969 likely comes from adding components to address unverified parts. The report has 137 lines, suggesting a need to note potential catastrophic cost underestimates, like heat exchangers at £2.96.**Assessing cost discrepancies**\n\nI'm noticing that the target raw cost of £1800 was only achieved due to poor scaling and misclassification. It's puzzling because the brief states that the maximum OEM unit cost should be £1,800. However, the raw Bill of Materials (BoM) shows £1801, while the OEM transfer cost of £2283.56 exceeds this target. There seems to be a significant discrepancy that needs to be addressed for accurate financial assessment.**Evaluating refrigeration safety**\n\nI need to address some ventilation issues and calculate the charge for a 0.8 kg R290 system, considering leak sensors. I’ve seen evidence about charge limits per occupied area, but without proper calculations. If there’s a leak, the flammable cloud risk is concerning, especially with the pressure details. The MAWP seems too low for R290, and I need to check for critical pressures and missing relief valves in the design. Overall, there seem to be significant risks that require further findings.**Analyzing compressor compatibility**\n\nI’m looking into the liquid receiver for a full charge pump-down with a 0.8 kg propane system. While outdoor service might be fine, the receiver definitely needs a pressure relief valve. The chosen compressor, YP0135E-TFD-290, is supposed to be compatible, but its fabrication status is still unverified from the appendix. There’s a mention of using R290-rated compressors, but even with chemistry hazards addressed, electrical incompatibilities still pose a concern that needs to be resolved.**Analyzing cost discrepancies**\n\nIt seems the report doesn’t list £4969 due to issues with the cost regression investigation, which is blocked by an inconsistent cost layer. I find quotes of £1801, but the appendix remains unverified. I need to fix the summary aggregator to use the report's cost layer rather than a